    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a deck build in Vancouver and need to double check my math on the load requirements. It's a cedar deck attached to a single-story house. Can you explain the difference between live load and dead load for me and how I should be approaching the calculations for post spacing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Live load represents the temporary weight on the deck, such as people, furniture, and snow, while dead load is the permanent weight of the structure itself, like the lumber and decking materials. When calculating post spacing, you must sum the expected dead load and the maximum required live load for the area being supported. Building codes specify minimum load requirements for different deck usages in Vancouver, so I strongly recommend referencing the specific BC building code for your design.",
      "source": "target",
