    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm looking at using 6x6 posts for the support. When I'm looking at the beam sizing to prevent sagging over time, what are the main factors that contribute to structural failure or unexpected weaknesses in the timber? I want to make sure the structure stays solid for years.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The primary contributors to timber weakness are improper species selection, structural defects like checks or knots, and inadequate connection design at beam-to-post junctures. Excessive deflection, which is sagging, is a performance failure related to insufficient beam sizing for the span and loading. To ensure longevity, use properly graded, pressure-treated lumber suited for exterior exposure and verify that all bolt and connection schedules meet engineering standards.",
      "source": "target",
